{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c355","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c3a4-a344-7cc0-aee2-78222c3c3de8"},"sequence":5,"recorded_at":1780531400000005,"record_type":"status","durability":"ephemeral","causation_id":"589b4e24-8b8d-450d-860d-37a0ee6fb543","payload_type":"turn.input.user","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"command_id":"589b4e24-8b8d-450d-860d-37a0ee6fb543","kind":"turn_input_user","prompt":"ROLE: CP25-PB-PROVE, adversarial mathematical subagent using Muse Spark 1.3. Work read-only on /mnt/c/Users/MDP/collatz-analysis/Collatz; scratch only /tmp/cp25-pb-prove. Print a self-contained report to stdout. Collatz is OPEN and must not be claimed solved.\n\nSETUP (exact):\nLet p>=3 be odd. Let w=(epsilon_0,...,epsilon_{m-1}) be a primitive binary word with k ones. Put M=2^m, P=p^k, D=M-P, b=sign(D), A=|D|. For each cyclic rotation r, let B_r=sum_{j:epsilon_{r+j}=1} 2^j p^{number of later ones in that rotation}. Let h_r be the unique representative in {1,...,M} satisfying h_r == -b B_r P^{-1} (mod M). Let q_r=B_r/A.\nThen h_r == A^{-1}B_r (mod M), and B_r=A h_r+M t_r for t_r in Z. C1 is: if t_r>=0 for all rotations, then A|B_0.\nThe exact cycle identity is 2q_{r+1}=p^{epsilon_r}q_r+b epsilon_r, hence product over epsilon_r=1 of (p+b/q_r)=M.\n\nChatGPT Web reduced C1 to this proposed stronger lemma:\nPRODUCT BARRIER (PB): assume A does not divide B_0. Define y_r=h_r+M/A. Then\n- if b=+1, product over epsilon_r=1 of (p+1/y_r) < M;\n- if b=-1, product over epsilon_r=1 of (p-1/y_r) > M.\nPB implies C1 by monotonicity because t_r>=1 gives q_r>=y_r.\n\nTASK:\n1) Check every definition and implication, with special attention to b=-1 positivity of factors and whether A|B is rotation-invariant.\n2) Prove PB without assuming C1/A|B, OR give an explicit exact counterexample (p,m,w,B,A,h,y,product comparison).\n3) If neither, reduce PB to one precise lemma. Derive a recurrence for h or y and a telescoping/product comparison; do not merely repeat the statement.\n4) Determine whether PB is genuinely stronger/non-circular or algebraically equivalent to C1.\n5) Output one verdict: PROOF, COUNTEREXAMPLE, OPEN-NONTRIVIAL, or TRIVIAL-CIRCULAR.\nFinite searches are evidence only. Include all equations needed for independent audit.","run_stream":{"id":"589b4e24-8b8d-450d-860d-37a0ee6fb543","kind":"run"}}}
